{"entries":{"chapter-31-study-disorders-of-swallowing|Chapter 31 Study: Disorders of Swallowing":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Dysphagia is difficulty with oral preparation or movement from mouth through esophagus. Presbyphagia describes healthy age-associated changes, not inherently impaired swallowing (p437).":["u3"],"copy|Prevalence is near 15% in community-dwelling/independent groups and upward of 40% in institutional populations (p437).":["u4"],"copy|Reduced reserve becomes clinically important with illness, frailty, altered cognition, medications, tubes or prolonged intubation (p442, p446).":["u5"],"copy|Ask about liquid, solid and pill difficulty, pocketing, prolonged meals and weight loss; distinguish primary swallowing dysfunction from other causes of slow eating (p446).":["u6"],"copy|Absence of cough does not exclude silent aspiration. Bedside findings may raise suspicion, but instrumental assessment is often needed (p448).":["u7"],"copy|Modified barium swallow shows dynamic anatomy, physiology and bolus flow and permits trials of posture and food/fluid adaptations (p448).":["u8"],"copy|FEES is portable and repeatable and permits real food assessment; the swallow itself is briefly obscured by “white-out” (p449).":["u9"],"copy|Combine speech-language pathology, dietary, medical and psychosocial input; align recommendations with the patient's goals (p447, p449).":["u10"],"copy|Compensatory strategies alter bolus flow or environment; rehabilitation targets muscle or neural function. The approaches may be combined (p449).":["u11"],"copy|In Parkinson disease/dementia trials, honey-thick fluids reduced immediate aspiration more than nectar-thick or chin-down strategies, but the trial found no significant difference in pneumonia incidence between chin-down and thickened fluids over 3 months (p450; Robbins et al., PMID 18378947).":["u12"],"copy|Free-water protocols require oral care and careful selection; small studies in ambulatory, cognitively intact rehabilitation patients cannot establish safety for all older adults (p450).":["u13"],"copy|Promote oral hygiene and review medication formulations and dysphagia-provoking drugs. Pills taken lying down or with too little fluid can damage the esophagus (p451).":["u14"],"copy|A swallowing test is only one part of a feeding decision; autonomy, dignity, quality of life and tube-related harms matter (p451).":["u15"],"copy|The chapter finds no evidence that feeding tubes prolong survival in dementia with dysphagia, and favors approaches such as hand feeding in that population (p451).":["u16"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u17"],"table-31-5-book-image|Table 31-5 — book image":["u18"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 31-5, p.446":["u19","u32"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u20"],"copy|2025-Jun Q71":["u21","u30"],"copy|CHAPTER 31s / HAZZARD":["u22"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 31 - Disorders of Swallowing; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u23"],"copy|Definition & risk":["u24"],"copy|Assessment":["u25"],"copy|Management":["u26"],"copy|Exam traps":["u27"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u28"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u29"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u31"],"copy|Question citing Table 31-5":["u31"]},"maxId":32}
